  <h2 id="layers">Three layers</h2>
  <ol class="layers">
    <li>
      <strong>Canon.</strong> One repository holds the policy, stated once: the operating system, the
      governance levels, the role charters, the Definition of Done, the review checklist and the templates.
      It knows nothing about any particular project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The delta.</strong> Each adopting repository carries one file,
      <code>llm/governance/governance-delta.md</code>. It binds the canon to that project: mission,
      design-authority document, the paths it uses, its check command, its L0 allowlist, what its platform
      can actually enforce, and whether the steward lane is switched on. It also pins the canon version, so a
      repository upgrades on purpose and never by accident.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Execution.</strong> A Claude Code plugin supplies four AI role agents, three skills
      (<code>/governance:establish</code>, <code>/governance:audit</code>, <code>/governance:migrate</code>)
      and a dependency-free checker, <code>governance-checks.mjs</code>. Specialist analysis is handed to the
      separate Constellize personas rather than duplicated.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The repository itself is split by role. Anything that governs, plans or records how the repository runs
    lives in <code>llm/</code>, the control plane. Deliverables, external material and generated views such
    as this page live in <code>docs/</code> or on a site, the data plane, and each view has to name the
    control-plane document it summarizes (<a href={`${canon}/llm/governance/adr/0001-llm-control-plane-docs-data-plane.md`}>ADR-0001</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="workflow">The workflow</h2>
  <p>
    Every piece of work takes the same path, whatever produced it. Nothing skips review except an explicitly
    authorized emergency repair.
